A||N||8||pollock||Intercooling?||||5||
Z||000000||pollock||05-12-2000||11:09 AM||||What does an intercooler do and how does it work?<P>Are they only used with turbos?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phantom Rage||05-12-2000||12:32 PM||||Try this:  <A HREF="http://minimopar.simplenet.com/perf/intercooler.html" TARGET=_blank>http://minimopar.simplenet.com/perf/intercooler.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-12-2000||06:49 PM||||Real short answer. It's a heat exchanger. They work just like the radiator. <P>It cools the air exiting a turbo/super charger. Automotive units are air to air. Marine units are air to air or air to water. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||05-13-2000||01:35 AM||||i have a 87 tbird turbo,<P>mine is used to cool the air before it enters the combustion chamber. Its this black thing that has fins like a radiatior with air passing through it to cool the air going to the intake.<P>The cooler the air, the more horsepower you can achieve.  Also, the colder the air, the more boost you can put in without it knocking.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phantom Rage||05-13-2000||08:39 AM||||Hey Orge I know you really know your stuff, that is a good short answer, but liquid to air intercoolers can be and has been used in auto applications.  The problem is that they are a bit more complicated to set up, requiring a seperate tank, pump, and radiator for the intercooler.  The main problem I think is the price, they cost a good bit more.  Spearco has a kit as I have described.  The good thing about these suckers are that they are tiny in size, These would work great in a Fiero where cool moving air is not readily available in the rear of the car.<BR>OK now, please don't crush me with a boulder Orge <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000005||FieroSTETZ||05-14-2000||03:54 AM||||what are those things called that (used in electronics applications) get hotter on one side and colder on the other when you pass current through them? could something like that be used? or maybe (my computer is water cooled) use an electric pump and thin copper tubing wrapped tightly around the pipe and rune icewater through it to chill the air?||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||05-14-2000||11:31 AM||||FieroSTETZ, you are thinking of a Peltier Junction.  Your description is dead on.  They<BR>thermally polarize the material by passing a current through the junction (heat on one side/cold on the other).  The trick would be to get enough surface area on the cold side to cool that large a volume of air.  They also tend to draw a lot of current so the HP gain due to the denser air would likely be less that the power it takes to generate the electricity at the alternator.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||05-14-2000||03:08 PM||||Fiero Motorsports just finished an air to water intercooled turbo w/water injection. It is built on a 3.4 pushrod V6. Charge is cooled down after the turbo by going through a custom intercooler that had to be mounted in the trunk. Next to it is a resevoir using ethyl glycol that is run to a motorcycle radiator being fed through a side intake on the passenger's side. At 10lbs of boost we have measured the delta (temp difference between charge going into intercooler and coming out) at a 75 degree difference. The water injection comes on at 7 lbs of boost. Its a great setup but way to complicated for me to want one.||||reg||1
Z||000008||pollock||05-15-2000||09:44 AM||||WKD, in your shop's little setup, what signals the water injection to come on?  Does your boost gauge have an output signal or something?<P>Thanks for all the replies.  I looked at that minimopar site-- answered my question thoroughly.<BR>||||reg||6
